\f0\fs24 \cf0 Liquid Alchemy Recipe Content Standards\
\
Purpose\
\
Liquid Alchemy aims to preserve not only recipes, but also the context, provenance, and understanding that make those recipes meaningful. Not every cocktail will have information available for every category below. Missing information is acceptable. The goal is to capture as much verified and useful information as is reasonably available.\
\
When referencing another named cocktail, place the relationship in the Riffs field whenever practical. Notes should primarily describe preparation, balance, ingredients, technique, service, or usage of the current cocktail. This helps preserve clean search behavior and creates explicit cocktail-to-cocktail relationships.\
\
A recipe should never be excluded because some fields are unknown.\
\
---\
\
Required Fields\
\
These fields should be present for every cocktail entry.\
\
Name\
\
Family\
\
Subfamily\
\
Recipe\
- Ingredients\
- Amounts\
- Units\
\
Technique\
\
Garnish\
\
Basic Lore\
- At minimum, a short description or explanation that helps the user understand the drink.\
\
---\
\
Strongly Recommended Fields\
\
These fields should be included whenever reasonably available.\
\
Creator\
- Individual creator, bar, organization, or publication associated with the drink.\
\
Source\
- Book, website, article, video, publication, or other source.\
\
Source URL\
- Link to the original source when available.\
\
Why It Matters\
- Brief explanation of why the cocktail is notable, influential, unusual, historically important, technically interesting, or simply worth knowing.\
\
Notes\
- Technique notes, ingredient guidance, substitutions, service notes, or other useful information.\
\
---\
\
Provenance Fields\
\
Liquid Alchemy places special emphasis on provenance.\
\
Every cocktail should attempt to answer:\
\
"Where did this drink come from?"\
\
Provenance may take several forms:\
\
Historical Provenance\
- Creator\
- Bar\
- Publication\
- Date created\
\
Structural Provenance\
- Family lineage\
- Related cocktails\
- Architectural ancestors\
\
Ingredient Provenance\
- Ingredient innovations\
- Defining ingredients\
- Signature flavor relationships\
\
Personal Provenance\
- House creations\
- Event creations\
- Family recipes\
- Personal favorites\
- Creator cocktails\
\
Creator attribution should be included whenever it can be reasonably determined and verified.\
\
Creator attribution lends credibility to a recipe and pays homage to the people who created the cocktail.\
\
---\
\
Optional Enrichment Fields\
\
These fields add depth but are not required.\
\
Lore\
- Historical context\
- Stories\
- Interesting background\
\
Riffs\
- Variations\
- Descendants\
- Related cocktails\
\
Alchemy Insight\
- Educational observations\
- Structural lessons\
- Technique insights\
- Ingredient relationships\
\
Craft Links\
- Homemade ingredients\
- Syrups\
- Infusions\
- Cordials\
- Preparations\
\
Tasting Notes\
- Flavor profile\
- Texture\
- Aroma\
- Finish\
\
Related Cocktails\
- Similar drinks\
- Ancestors\
- Descendants\
\
Creator Notes\
- Information about the creator\
- Additional creator context\
\
---\
\
Editorial Philosophy\
\
Not every cocktail has a rich documented history.\
\
Not every cocktail comes from a famous creator.\
\
Not every cocktail changed cocktail culture.\
\
Every cocktail comes from somewhere.\
\
Whenever possible, Liquid Alchemy seeks to preserve:\
\
- How to make the drink\
- Why the drink works\
- Where the drink came from\
- Who created it\
- What it connects to\
\
The goal is not merely to collect recipes.\
\
The goal is to create a resource that rewards curiosity and deepens understanding.}